  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 100 AM MDT Thu Sep 24 2026\n\nIR currently depicts mainly clear skies across the CWA with a\nlow well to the southeast over the Kansas high plains spinning\nsome high clouds into southeastern portions of the state. A\ndeepening trough off the western Canadian coast is extending\nsouth towards the Pacific northwest dropping the main finger of\nthe PFJ southward as seen on WV. This will bring a series of\nshortwaves across the CWA by Saturday morning when a cold front\npushes in and stalls, remaining until pushes through by Sunday\nnight. \n\nBefore that, expect a tranquil Thursday in regards to sunny\nskies, relatively light winds, and dry conditions. Temperatures\nwill be a bit warmer than Wednesday, extending a slight warming\ntrend into Friday as well. Stronger winds Friday afternoon ahead\nof the aforementioned cold front, strengthening the pressure\ngradient through much of the CWA and especially for points\nacross the wind corridor and higher elevations. Nothing\nextraordinary, but gusts up to 25 to 30 mph can be had Friday\nand extending into Saturday. In terms of the cold front, more\nseasonable temperatures Saturday and Sunday as it stalls about\nhalfway through the CWA, dependent on what model one looks at as\nthere is not much agreement on where it does and how far it\npushes eastward. Regardless, a mid level trough to the southeast\nis trending further away from the CWA, keeping any precipitation\nFriday night and Saturday out of the area into southeastern \nparts of the state and into Colorado and the high plains. \n\nThe main trough will extend through the CWA by late Sunday and\ninto early next week as the PFJ dips into Montana brining\nprecipitation chances to the northwest and northern mountains\nSunday night and becoming more widespread for Monday onward. The\nEC is trending to a closed low over California by Monday and \ninto Tuesday that will slowly push eastward to the south of the \nstate that will bring the more moisture rich, southwest flow for\nthe CWA. This is more conducive for precipitation chances to \nincrease for the much of the area, especially east of the Divide\nthat tends to be blocked with more of a westerly flow. Models \npush this low/trough far enough east by Thursday with weak \npositively tilted ridging to build in from the southwest by \nweek's end to dry things out. Time will tell on which model \nhandles this low better, even with the EC being better long term,\nit still tends to be slower than its output. Stay tuned for \nupdated forecasts in the coming days.",
